Lesson Summary
Cryptography is the science of encrypting and decrypting data. Confusion is one of the elements, and it prevents attackers from looking for patterns between ciphertext and plaintext.
Data states are "at rest" (stored in various media), "in transit" (data that moves from medium to medium) and "in use" (data that is currently being created, deleted, read or modified).
A digital signature is a message digest that has been encrypted again with a user's private key.
There are two types of ciphers, stream cipher (encrypts data one bit at a time) and block cipher (encrypts data in blocks).
Self-signed is a type of certificate that does not recognise higher authority in the chain. It is owned by the same entity that signs it and it requires the client to trust the entity directly. Other types of certificates are root user, computer, email, code signing, SAN, wildcard, DV and EV.
Private keys have to be protected because they are crucial to the security of the hierarchy. They need to be deleted from insecure media, backed up to removable media, and never shared.
